    measured_cc_heat_rates: { cell: "U", ev: "SEEDED by lane SOCO-57 (2026-09-20), the PR that adds this mechanism — a mechanical emission under rule 28(c) (a new solve-affecting field lands its base row plus a cell line in EVERY shard in one PR), NO VERDICT MINTED and NOTHING TRANSFERRED. Rule 25 [R-ISO-SCOPE] / 28(d): SOCO's measurement fills no other ISO's cell. This ISO has no campd_cc_heat_rates_<ISO>.csv at this commit, so the field is a STRICT NO-OP here — armed or not, every CC_REGULAR row keeps its eGRID rate. To move this cell an ISO lane runs scripts/data/derive_campd_cc_heat_rates.py --iso <ISO> over its OWN plants and solves its own span. NOTE THE BOUNDARY GUARD before assuming coverage: at some sites CAMPD meters the combustion turbines but not the unfired steam generator, and those plants are refused rather than applied, so an ISO's applied coverage is not its CC capacity." },
    measured_coal_heat_rates: { cell: "U", ev: "SEEDED by lane NWPP-42 (2026-09-19), the PR that adds this mechanism — a mechanical emission under rule 28(c) (a new solve-affecting field lands its base row plus a cell line in EVERY shard in one PR), NO VERDICT MINTED and NOTHING TRANSFERRED. Rule 25 [R-ISO-SCOPE] / 28(d): NWPP's measurement fills no other ISO's cell. This ISO has no campd_coal_heat_rates_<ISO>.csv at this commit, so the field is a STRICT NO-OP here — armed or not, every coal row keeps its eGRID rate. To move this cell an ISO lane runs scripts/data/derive_campd_coal_heat_rates.py --iso <ISO> over its OWN plants and solves its own span." },
    measured_st_heat_rates: { cell: "U", ev: "SEEDED by lane SOCO-53e (2026-09-19), the PR that adds this mechanism — a mechanical emission under rule 28(c) (a new solve-affecting field lands its base row plus a cell line in EVERY shard in one PR), NO VERDICT MINTED and NOTHING TRANSFERRED. Rule 25 [R-ISO-SCOPE] / 28(d): SOCO's measurement fills no other ISO's cell. This ISO has no campd_st_heat_rates_<ISO>.csv at this commit, so the field is a STRICT NO-OP here — armed or not, every gas-steam row keeps its eGRID rate. To move this cell an ISO lane runs scripts/data/derive_campd_gas_st_heat_rates.py --iso <ISO> over its OWN plants and solves its own span. WORTH READING FIRST: the repair bites hardest where a COAL boiler and a GAS boiler share one ORIS code, because the prime-mover-family rate blends them too — an ISO with such a site (PJM, MISO and NWPP all have several) is the natural next candidate." },
    measured_ct_heat_rates: { cell: "K", ev: "pjm-137; FINDING-pjm137-dominion-congestion-is-subzonal-2026-07-29; re-gated pjm-150 (results/calibration/FINDING-pjm150-ct-heat-rate-regate-2026-08-03.md) — keeper already on the corrected artifact via pjm-147, K2 bit-identical CELL UNCHANGED, RE-MEASURED NOT RE-OPENED \u2014 pjm-h1 (2026-09-12, docs/FINDING-pjm-h1-cc-cf-does-not-track-2026-09-12.md; ZERO LP, no arm, no solve). FALSIFIED AS A PJM CC_REGULAR LEVER, so no successor spends an LP on it. The object it would address: CC_REGULAR is the ONLY PJM class whose matched-fleet ANNUAL CAPACITY FACTOR does not track the CAMPD meter across 2020-2025 (cross-year r = \u22120.183, model CF range 0.015 against the meter\u2019s 0.058), while COAL_BIT +0.912, CT_PEAKER +0.896, ST_GAS +0.968 and CC_CHP +0.775 all do \u2014 and the two flattest years are exactly the two C1 failures (2021 +26.31, 2022 +22.56 TWh). AND THE NEIGHBOURING CC HYPOTHESIS IS FALSIFIED TOO, WHICH IS WHY IT IS RECORDED HERE: CC_REGULAR has no measured-heat-rate artifact where this cell (K) and measured_chp_heat_rates (K) both do, and it carries the eGRID plant-average ANNUAL rate \u2014 the exact defect rule 14 was applied to fix for CT and CHP. That asymmetry is real and it is NOT the answer. Measured on CAMPD heatInput/grossLoad over 4,756,608 unit-hours on all 70 PJM CC_REGULAR bench plants, 2023-2025, operating hours only: fleet energy-weighted GROSS 6.9889 MMBtu/MWh (net \u2248 7.146 at a stated 2.2 % own-use). Paired against the model\u2019s own fleet loader at the keeper\u2019s heat-rate flags, 69 plants: capacity-weighted model 7.1954 vs measured-net 7.2974, i.e. the model is 1.40 % CHEAP; median per-plant delta +0.008; and 39 of 69 plants are DEARER in the model than in the meter. The fleet rate is right and a `measured_cc_heat_rates` candidate must NOT be built on this premise (per-plant outliers 55976/55337/55710, 3.3-4.1 MMBtu/MWh cheap, are a separate and much smaller question). Reported against the hypothesis this session opened with. The surviving explanation \u2014 price-distribution compression (D-A amplitude 29.9-32.9 % of measured in EVERY year) pinning a large mid-merit class online 78-83 % of hours at 72-76 % loading, invariantly \u2014 is inside PJM\u2019s owner-declared-closed price-formation frontier (pjm-142) and is ROUTED to the standing pjm-159 escalation, not pulled as a lever. It now carries a refutable prediction it did not have: an amplitude repair that leaves CC_REGULAR\u2019s cross-year CF correlation at \u2248 0 has not addressed it." },
