    ercot_partial_outage_shaped_derate: { cell: ".", ev: "CELL UNCHANGED, RE-MEASURED NOT RE-OPENED \u2014 pjm-h1 (2026-09-12, docs/FINDING-pjm-h1-cc-cf-does-not-track-2026-09-12.md; ZERO LP, no arm, no solve). FALSIFIED AS A PJM CC_REGULAR LEVER, so no successor spends an LP on it. The object it would address: CC_REGULAR is the ONLY PJM class whose matched-fleet ANNUAL CAPACITY FACTOR does not track the CAMPD meter across 2020-2025 (cross-year r = \u22120.183, model CF range 0.015 against the meter\u2019s 0.058), while COAL_BIT +0.912, CT_PEAKER +0.896, ST_GAS +0.968 and CC_CHP +0.775 all do \u2014 and the two flattest years are exactly the two C1 failures (2021 +26.31, 2022 +22.56 TWh). THE CAPABILITY HYPOTHESIS IS DEAD ON PJM\u2019S OWN DATA: the meter\u2019s revealed capability (p99.5 hourly MW / nameplate, fleet mean) is FLAT at 0.8935/0.9024/0.9121/0.9103/0.9051/0.9029 \u2014 2022 is the HIGHEST of the six years \u2014 so there is no 2021/2022 capability collapse for a derate to find. Independently, the committed data/raw/campd-partial-outages-shaped.csv carries ZERO rows on any PJM bench plant (1,609 CC_REGULAR + 2,647 COAL rows, all ERCOT): arming this for PJM would be a derive job pointed at a falsified object. Cell stays `.`; rule 28(d) unaffected \u2014 ERCOT\u2019s own verdict is ERCOT\u2019s." },
